  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Sunday)\nIssued at 1135 AM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nUpper trough lifting NE across the region continues to support \nwidespread rain and some snow across western SD. Bulk of rain and \nsnow will fall today, with precip ending this evening as the \nsystem lifts out. Areas of fog will continue through tonight as \nmoist saturated ESE flow persists. Winds will also increase \ntonight as pressure falls ensue ahead of the next impulse, \ntightening the pressure gradient behind the departing sfc high. \nExpect breezy winds most areas tonight. Winds will shift SW as a \nsfc low moves across the region Tue. This will allow things to \nwarm up quickly Tue as dry downslope SW flow spreads over the FA. \nThe strongest winds will be across far SW SD and portions of \nnortheast WY. Very dry air will move into the region as dewpoints \nfall into the teens across northeast WY into SW SD, supporting \nelevated to near critical fire weather conditions there. Much \nwarmer Tue with highs in the 60s. A few 70s may be possible across\nfar SW SD. Mean upper troughing over the PAC NW and western \nCanada will support general SW flow through the remainder week \nwith weak impulses traversing the flow. Expect mainly dry weather \nweather as these systems fight very dry low levels, although a few\nsprinkles may be possible as each weak shortwave traverses the \nregion. Highs will generally be in the 60s, with thermal \npotentials depending on the timing of the thermal ridges ahead \neach system. This could allow slightly warmer or cooler numbers \nthan what the current forecast has. Upper trough will lift out the\nPac NW early next week, with slightly better chances for precip \nand cooler temps for a day or two.",
